# Lesson 24 — Statistical vs. Business Significance

## Key Concepts
Statistical significance asks whether an observed effect is unlikely to be explained by random variation under the null hypothesis.

Business significance asks whether the size of that effect is meaningful enough to justify action.

## Example
An experiment can produce a statistically significant 0.1% conversion lift with a very large sample. That does not automatically mean the change is worth launching if implementation cost, risk, or operational complexity outweighs the incremental value.

## Decision Framework
Evaluate:
- Effect size
- Confidence interval
- Sample size
- Revenue or cost impact
- Implementation cost
- Customer impact
- Strategic importance

## Interview Takeaway
Never stop at `p < 0.05`. Translate the result into business impact and make a recommendation based on both statistical evidence and practical value.
